From b6d8bf3cea424c56289a07dfa02af93996ed440e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: root <root@srv1843077.hstgr.cloud>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:39:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] =?UTF-8?q?hud=5Fweb:=20address=20sol's=20review=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=94=20all=20twelve=20findings?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Independent cross-model review (gpt-5.6-sol) returned "request changes"
with twelve findings. I confirmed the top four against the source myself
before touching anything; all twelve are addressed here.

High:

/frame.png staged through a fixed path under the OS temp directory, which
on Unix was always /tmp/ezquake-hudweb.png. fopen follows symlinks, so
another local user could pre-create that path pointing at any file the
player can write and have opening the editor clobber it with PNG data;
the file also lingered afterwards, world-readable under a normal umask,
holding a picture of the player's screen. Added Image_EncodePNG, which
runs the same libpng settings straight into a heap block, and removed the
file entirely.

Medium:

`togglehud` is gone from the allowlist. Despite the name it falls back
from HUD elements to any cvar and toggles it (hud.c:498), so it let
`togglehud rcon_password` walk straight past the cvar-prefix policy. The
editor sets hud_<name>_show and never needed it.

/cmd now refuses `$`. Cbuf_ExecuteEx runs Cmd_ExpandString before
dispatch (cmd.c:1865) — after this code has approved the line — so
`hud_tracking_format $rcon_password` passed the hud_ prefix, expanded
into the secret, and /state handed it back. That turned a HUD-scoped
token into arbitrary cvar disclosure.

An allowlisted command must now also exist as a command. `fontload` is
only registered with EZ_FREETYPE_SUPPORT (fonts.c:526); without it the
dispatcher falls through to aliases (cmd.c:1948), so an allowlisted name
the build does not provide would have run whatever alias sat under it.

Frame capture is rate limited to one per hud_web_frame_interval (250ms
default), shared across clients, since the cost is the engine's and not
each client's. PROTOCOL.md claimed capture wrote "to memory instead of
disk", which was false until this commit; it now states the real cost.

geometry.js used physical_width/vid_width for BOTH axes. ezQuake lets
vid_conwidth and vid_conheight be set independently (vid_sdl2.c:1928), so
every handle was misplaced vertically whenever the console shape differed
from the screen's. My own QA could not have caught it: at 512x288 on
2560x1440 both ratios are 5.

radar ships width "30%" / height "25%" and resolves them against the
console (hud_radar.c:1307). Number("30%") is NaN, so `|| 0` made it zero
and the first corner drag rewrote a working radar as a small absolute
number. Percentage-sized elements are now a distinct mode with no
handles and a note saying why.

Colour parsing did not match the engine. It rounded and clamped where
Q_atoi truncates and the byte cast wraps, so "1.9" read as palette 2
instead of 1 and "256 0 0" as red instead of black; it rejected values
with more than four tokens, which StringToRGB_W simply ignores; and it
accepted colour names everywhere, when ColorNameToRGBString is installed
only by specific callbacks and frame_color is the only HUD one
(hud.c:924) — so a bar colour of "red" is palette 0, not red. The commit
that added the picker claimed swatches show what the engine draws; now
they do.

`face` and `iammo` are POV-gated in their draw functions but carried no
HUD_NEEDS_POV, so the editor did not know. Added. `drawn` genuinely
cannot mean "pixels appeared" — HUD_PrepareDraw stamps the sequence
before the caller draws, and several elements call it before their own
condition — so the comment now says what it actually means rather than
overclaiming.

A long enough numeric cvar overflows Q_atof to infinity, and `%g` emitted
`"pos_x":inf`, which is not JSON and wedged the editor's polling. Numeric
fields go through sb_json_number, which clamps to finite.

Low:

LIBHUD_NO_GROW was 1<<3; HUD_NO_GROW is 1<<9. The tests passed only
because they used the same wrong constant on both sides.

A placement cycle made every element in it vanish from the tree: each had
a present parent, so none became a root and the depth guard never ran.
Cycles are now detected and shown at the root.

The font list matched its extension as an unanchored regex, offering
"foo.ttf.bak", and offered names with spaces that `fontload` cannot
accept (fonts.c:441). Both are filtered.

Verified: libhud tests 30/30, generated assets fresh, colour semantics
covered by eight new cases against the engine's rules.
